WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.420
Road Kill Easy Bake Oven, The Clash at Demonhead,&nbsp;
and a store of unicorns. From Indie darling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:11.220
Oscar Winner to Marvel superhero, Brie&nbsp;
Larson's transformation is stunning!

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.860
Brie Larson, born Brianne Desaulniers,&nbsp;
expressed an interest in acting at age six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.080
later moving to Los Angeles with her mom after her&nbsp;
parents got divorced. Within a year, she became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.520
the youngest actor to enroll in the legendary&nbsp;
American Conservatory Theater in San Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.160
where actors like Denzel Washington and Winona&nbsp;
Ryder cut their teeth. Larson attributes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.760
early ambition to her mother, claiming&nbsp;
that her maternal grandparents, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.534
"wouldn't allow her to have that kind&nbsp;
of life," according to Elle Magazine.

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:44.160
After adopting the last name "Larson" from her&nbsp;
great-grandmother in an effort to appeal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.900
casting directors, Brie began to regularly&nbsp;
book roles. The first of those was for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.120
commercial parody on "The Tonight Show with&nbsp;
Jay Leno" in 1996, promoting "Malibu Mudslide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.860
Barbie." Thankfully, Larson was charming enough&nbsp;
as a child actor to be invited back for over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.317
dozen more parodies like the "Road Kill Easy&nbsp;
Bake Oven." She told Jimmy Fallon in 2017:

00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:06.481
"It's kind of where I learned&nbsp;
I had comedic timing."

00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:11.820
That comedic timing would later come in handy&nbsp;
for Larson as she began to book more roles on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.420
becoming a series regular on "Raising&nbsp;
Dad," where she played the daughter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.320
Bob Saget's character during the&nbsp;
show's single-season run in 2001.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.000
While Larson was still far from becoming an&nbsp;
Oscar-winning movie star, her time in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.380
spotlight would come sooner than she thought.&nbsp;
As her child acting career slowly continued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.880
Larson eventually recorded a song, "Invisible&nbsp;
Girl," and uploaded it to her website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.720
where a local Los Angeles radio&nbsp;
station picked it up. At age 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.080
Larson was offered a record&nbsp;
deal by Casablanca Records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.940
giving her the same shot at stardom as teens&nbsp;
of the time like Taylor Swift or Miley Cyrus.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.960
"Hey, this is Brie Larson. Welcome&nbsp;
to Sessions at AOL. Come on in!"

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.420
Larson's record label had her quickly record&nbsp;
her debut album, "Finally Out of P.E.," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.980
after several delays was finally released&nbsp;
in fall 2005. The album's lead single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.360
"She Said," was a minor hit on the Billboard&nbsp;
charts. The album's thirteenth and final track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.860
"Hope Has Wings," was featured in "Barbie and&nbsp;
the Magic of Pegasus," which Larson promoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.060
by performing on the "Barbie" float during&nbsp;
the 2005 Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.740
Although Larson clearly had the potential to&nbsp;
reach an audience of angsty mid-2000s teens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.640
the pop star life wasn't meant to be. In&nbsp;
hindsight, everything seems to have worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.300
out for the best, with Larson claiming in&nbsp;
an interview with Buzzfeed News in 2015,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.600
"[I was] so insecure and so&nbsp;
hard on myself back then."

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.971
Her musical side wouldn't get&nbsp;
sidelined for long, though.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.900
There was a lot riding on "United States of Tara,"&nbsp;
a 2009 Showtime series set to star Toni Collette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.700
as a woman with dissociative identity disorder.&nbsp;
Brie auditioned to play Collette's character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.900
daughter Kate in the pilot, allowing her to&nbsp;
potentially act alongside her greatest acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.520
hero, but unfortunately, she didn't land the&nbsp;
part. That is, until the show was picked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.873
and Kate was recast, giving Brie another shot&nbsp;
at the part, which she eventually locked down.

00:02:55.873 --> 00:02:59.520
Ironically, this finally paired Brie&nbsp;
with the show's creator, Diablo Cody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.500
who had penned "Juno." Being a series regular on&nbsp;
a Showtime series for three seasons gave Larson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.600
a lot of opportunities, such as the 2009&nbsp;
comedy film "Tanner Hall" and even a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.620
in "Our Town" for the Williamstown Theatre&nbsp;
Festival. Amongst all these other projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.200
however, Larson felt closest to Kate from "United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.395
States of Tara," who is rebellious and&nbsp;
selfish towards her struggling mother.

00:03:20.395 --> 00:03:24.060
Although critics had lots of praise for the&nbsp;
show, particularly for the performances of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.580
both Collette and Larson, it was canceled by&nbsp;
Showtime after its third season in 2011. In an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.480
interview with Buzzfeed, Larson lamented the&nbsp;
show's cancellation and regrets not spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.380
more time on her character's bedroom set&nbsp;
during their final days of shooting, saying,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.289
"I didn't say my final good-byes to those walls."

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.720
After "United States of Tara," Larson's jobs&nbsp;
started to get more high-profile. In 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.100
she co-starred in Noah Baumbach's "Greenberg"&nbsp;
alongside Ben Stiller and Greta Gerwig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.580
which helped raise her profile among&nbsp;
independent film fans. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.080
she made quite an impression on mainstream&nbsp;
audiences when she was cast in Edgar Wright's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.815
2010 film "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World,"&nbsp;
adapted from the graphic novel series.

00:04:02.815 --> 00:04:06.960
If Larson had struggled to grab the attention&nbsp;
of casting directors before, that changed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.220
her "Scott Pilgrim" audition. According to Edgar&nbsp;
Wright in an interview with Entertainment Weekly,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.680
"Brie — who was 19 at the time, maybe she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.668
even 18 when she auditioned&nbsp;
— blew everybody else away."

00:04:16.801 --> 00:04:20.340
That's certainly the energy Wright&nbsp;
wanted from the character of Envy Adams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.360
the ex-girlfriend of Michael Cera's Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim and lead singer of the successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.920
rock band The Clash at Demonhead, making good&nbsp;
use of Larson's experience as a musician.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:32.400
Even though "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World"&nbsp;
didn't perform well at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.260
its cult following has kept her portrayal&nbsp;
of Envy Adams alive, with Vulture heralding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.820
The Clash at Demonhead as one of the best fake&nbsp;
rock bands in cinematic history. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.500
Larson will be returning to the role with the rest&nbsp;
of the film's cast for a Netflix animated series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.609
proving that Larson's performance&nbsp;
is as irreplaceable as it gets.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.440
Even though Larson could've easily chased&nbsp;
the Envy Adams fame, her career naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.220
gravitated towards independent films.&nbsp;
Following "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.720
Larson starred in the drama film "Rampart"&nbsp;
opposite Woody Harrelson. This time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.380
the connection she felt to her character, like&nbsp;
the one she had with Kate from "United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:12.360
of Tara," didn't showcase Larson's best habits&nbsp;
as an actor. As she told The Los Angeles Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.740
the consequence of playing the emotionally&nbsp;
intense role meant it took her three times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.340
as long to get out of that depression&nbsp;
as it did to just do the scenes.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.840
It wasn't long before Larson became an indie&nbsp;
darling, even with films like an adaptation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.980
Douglas Light's book "The Trouble with Bliss,"&nbsp;
which critics felt was a step down from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:33.000
source material. Around this time, Larson&nbsp;
also dipped her toe into responsibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.880
behind the camera. In 2012, she co-wrote and&nbsp;
co-directed the short film "The Arm" with Jessie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.200
Ennis and Sarah Ramos, which was awarded the&nbsp;
Special Jury Prize at Sundance that year. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.948
was becoming clear to the industry as a whole&nbsp;
that Larson was someone to keep their eye on.

00:05:47.948 --> 00:05:52.380
The comedic timing Larson perfected on "The&nbsp;
Tonight Show with Jay Leno" didn't go to waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.880
during these indie darling years, either.&nbsp;
Larson played a prominent role in the 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.520
studio comedy "21 Jump Street," based on the late&nbsp;
1980s TV show of the same name. This time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.060
Larson's talent didn't go unnoticed,&nbsp;
with one reviewer for Slate, writing,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.680
"Brie Larson [...] is a find of major&nbsp;
proportions as Schmidt's goofy love interest."

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.520
She also guest-starred in a couple&nbsp;
of episodes of Seasons 4 and 5 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.800
NBC's "Community" as Rachel, a romantic&nbsp;
interest for Danny Pudi's TV-obsessed Abed.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.440
However, Brie's breakout role came as a result of&nbsp;
her dramatic talent, with Destin Daniel Cretton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.380
"Short Term 12" in 2013. After an audition over&nbsp;
Skype, Larson was cast as Grace, the supervisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.240
for a home of troubled teens who is also dealing&nbsp;
with her own unresolved trauma. The film contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.180
many soon-to-be stars of the late 2010s, including&nbsp;
Rami Malek, LaKeith Stanfield, and Kaitlyn Dever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.080
but it speaks to Larson's powerful performance&nbsp;
that she's the standout of the film.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.820
"Short Term 12" quickly became Larson's most&nbsp;
notable performance, even being nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.440
for an Independent Spirit Award in 2014.&nbsp;
A reviewer for The Telegraph correctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.489
predicted Larson's eventual success,&nbsp;
writing of her acting in "Short Term 12",

00:06:59.489 --> 00:07:01.920
"Larson in particular gives a performance (sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.280
sad and strong) that marks&nbsp;
her out for a stellar career."

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.857
However, not even they could've anticipated&nbsp;
what would come next for Larson.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:16.020
2015 became a turning point for Larson's career in&nbsp;
Hollywood, as she became an even more recognizable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.266
star. In the years leading up to 2015, her&nbsp;
profile began to grow with minor appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.266 --> 00:07:24.120
in films like appearances in films like "The&nbsp;
Spectacular Now," "Don Jon," and "The Gambler,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.160
all of which continued to earn her acclaim as&nbsp;
an up-and-coming star. Still, Larson was eager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.780
to showcase the comedic timing she had been so&nbsp;
proud of learning years ago as a child actor.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:38.700
One of these efforts was "Digging for Fire," a&nbsp;
comedy starring Jake Johnson and Rosemarie DeWitt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:43.680
as a couple who find evidence of a dead body in&nbsp;
their backyard and rope in their close friends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:49.080
investigate. The film notably lacked a script. It&nbsp;
was shot over 15 days based on a two-page outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.240
written by Johnson and director Joe Swanberg, with&nbsp;
actors encouraged to improvise dialogue and come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.560
up with their characters' backstories. Larson's&nbsp;
role as Max was originally intended to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.940
romantic chemistry with Johnson's, but Larson's&nbsp;
own contributions to the story nixed that.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.220
Also in 2015, Larson starred in Amy Schumer's&nbsp;
"Trainwreck" as the lead actress' sister. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:13.080
Schumer's character struggles with commitment in&nbsp;
relationships, Larson plays devil's advocate as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.580
happily married woman. In fact, her husband in&nbsp;
the film is played by comedian Mike Birbiglia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:23.100
who also starred alongside Larson in "Digging for&nbsp;
Fire," keeping her in the company of good comedy.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.420
While "Digging for Fire" was liked by critics&nbsp;
and "Trainwreck" was a box office success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:33.120
Larson's other film in 2015 earned the actress&nbsp;
her best reviews yet. That film was "Room,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.580
directed by Lenny Abrahamson and based on&nbsp;
Emma Donoghue's 2010 book. In the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.300
Larson stars as Joy, a young woman&nbsp;
who is held captive for seven years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:46.200
bearing her captor's son during&nbsp;
that time, played by Jacob Tremblay.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.280
Like many of her previous roles, Larson&nbsp;
committed fully to getting into the mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.580
of this incredibly traumatic role.&nbsp;
With the help of a nutritionist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.260
Larson adopted a strict diet to portray&nbsp;
Joy and isolated herself in her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.189
apartment. Describing the production&nbsp;
process, she told Vancouver City News,

00:09:02.189 --> 00:09:06.180
"It took a lot to recoup at the end&nbsp;
of the day. I needed a lot of sleep."

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.300
Even though this proved to be an&nbsp;
exhausting endeavor for the actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.534
it paid off in dividends, with critics raving&nbsp;
about her and Tremblay's harrowing performances.

00:09:14.534 --> 00:09:18.360
The film was an awards darling&nbsp;
in the 2015-2016 season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.100
with Larson winning the Academy Award&nbsp;
for Best Actress at only 26 years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:27.600
In her acceptance speech, she took the moment&nbsp;
to thank audiences for their acceptance of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.800
stressing the importance of movie fans in the&nbsp;
film industry. For someone at the pinnacle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:36.360
of fully-fledged movie stardom, Larson&nbsp;
expressed a warm humility in this speech.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:40.140
"Room" made Larson a household&nbsp;
name seemingly overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.180
as she also secured a Golden Globe and BAFTA win&nbsp;
for her performance in the movie. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:49.860
she was hesitant to accept a similar role or&nbsp;
be pigeonholed by playing a traumatized mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:55.260
instead opting to embrace her newfound status&nbsp;
as an international movie star. In 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.680
she hosted "Saturday Night Live,"&nbsp;
once again proving that even as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:02.520
an acclaimed dramatic actress, she&nbsp;
possessed unparalleled comedic talent.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.500
Larson continued to embrace her potential as a&nbsp;
movie star, following up "Room" with the 2017&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:13.440
film "Kong: Skull Island." In it, she plays Mason&nbsp;
Weaver, an investigative photojournalist who joins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.600
the expedition to find the legendary King Kong&nbsp;
in an attempt to expose the cruelty of humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.340
towards nature. Unlike previous "King Kong" films,&nbsp;
Larson's character isn't defined as the beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:28.680
damsel-in-distress who gets kidnapped by the giant&nbsp;
ape. In an interview with Vanity Fair, she said,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.920
"I thought it was fun that I didn't&nbsp;
have to be this pristine creature."

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.980
Some critics considered this&nbsp;
a step down for the actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.580
despite Larson's enthusiasm for the&nbsp;
film. An article for The Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.300
Post about actresses' struggles with&nbsp;
aging out of roles writes about Larson,

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.600
"One year you're winning an Oscar for&nbsp;
[...] an emotionally demanding drama;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.140
the next, you're [...] gasping your&nbsp;
way through a great big monkey movie."

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:53.820
But Larson would prove them wrong by showing&nbsp;
that she could be a strong blockbuster lead.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:59.340
Shortly after Brie Larson skyrocketed into&nbsp;
success following "Room," she was introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.634
at San Diego Comic-Con in 2016 as the latest&nbsp;
addition to the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:11:04.634 --> 00:11:09.614
"Captain Marvel herself, Brie Larson!"

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.060
Larson was set to star as Carol Danvers, the&nbsp;
very first female superhero to lead her own solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:23.580
film in the MCU with 2019's "Captain Marvel."&nbsp;
Despite Larson's eagerness to chase success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.580
the role was not without its&nbsp;
responsibilities: She said in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:30.660
an InStyle interview that she was attracted to&nbsp;
the role due to Danvers' strength, explaining,

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.000
"She's not ever shrinking herself down."

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.440
The film was also quite different from Marvel's&nbsp;
typical style at the time. Coming off the heels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:43.080
of 2018's superhero tragedy "Avengers: Infinity&nbsp;
War," "Captain Marvel" turned back the clock to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:48.480
the mid-1990s, when Carol Danvers becomes the&nbsp;
first superhero contact of Samuel L. Jackson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.940
Nick Fury, and eventually a hero for the refugee&nbsp;
shapeshifters known as Skrulls. The role required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:59.460
Larson to adopt a superhero physique in real-life,&nbsp;
involving a nine-month physical training regimen.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:03.060
Thankfully, Larson's effort paid off,&nbsp;
with critics praising the film for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:07.320
its originality amongst growing superhero&nbsp;
fatigue in the Marvel universe. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:11.160
to one review for The New Yorker, that's&nbsp;
all thanks to the film's heroine, stating,

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.601
"Praise be to Larson, for reminding us that&nbsp;
[superhero films] can be bringers of fun."

00:12:15.601 --> 00:12:19.620
Although the actress also faced sexist&nbsp;
criticism from some comic book fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:23.760
the praise for her role was&nbsp;
probably enough to tune it out.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:27.960
"Captain Marvel" marked the third on-screen&nbsp;
collaboration between Larson and Samuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:33.300
L. Jackson, as the two had previously starred&nbsp;
together in 2017's "Kong: Skull Island." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:38.520
long before "Captain Marvel" was even shot, Larson&nbsp;
recruited Jackson for her directorial debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:43.200
"Unicorn Store," which premiered at the&nbsp;
Toronto International Film Festival in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.580
where it was bought by Netflix and released&nbsp;
on their streaming service later in 2019.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:53.520
Along with directorial duties, Larson also stars&nbsp;
as Kit, an artist who is offered by a mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.920
salesman to own a unicorn if she accomplishes&nbsp;
a certain number of specific requirements. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.760
only was the role close to Larson's heart,&nbsp;
but it was a project that had been a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:08.040
time coming. Prior to signing on as director,&nbsp;
Larson auditioned for the film in 2012 but didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:12.300
land it. When it came back around following the&nbsp;
success of "Room" with the option to direct it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.292
she took it as her chance to show&nbsp;
Hollywood her untapped filmmaking skills.

00:13:16.292 --> 00:13:21.060
Unfortunately, critics were split on the final&nbsp;
product, with some praising its earnestness and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:26.100
depiction of femininity, while others criticized&nbsp;
its lack of consistent tone. However, some critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:30.180
correctly noted that Larson showed potential&nbsp;
behind the camera, with ScreenDaily writing,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.460
"Larson navigates through a cute story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.969
clear limitations to deliver a&nbsp;
film that's often quite funny."

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:40.260
Larson's career was on a steady incline&nbsp;
around the release of "Captain Marvel,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:45.300
with the actress reprising her role in 2019's&nbsp;
"Avengers: Endgame." However, everything came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.700
to a halt in 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic&nbsp;
forced Hollywood into shutdown. It ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:55.560
giving Larson a necessary break from acting.&nbsp;
Having turned 30 years old and noticing she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:00.240
was being offered lots of roles reminiscent&nbsp;
of "Room," she told Harper's Bazaar in 2023,

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.920
"I had all the same normal fears as everybody&nbsp;
else about being single and being 30."

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.140
Larson didn't spend the pandemic in hiding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:11.319
however, and chose instead to remain&nbsp;
closely connected to her online fanbase.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.840
"Hi, I'm Brie Larson…ugh, God. Hi."

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:21.360
This resulted in her starting her own YouTube&nbsp;
channel, where she posted workout vlogs, Q&amp;As,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:25.860
and even tours of her island in perhaps the&nbsp;
best quarantine video game, "Animal Crossing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:31.860
New Horizons." In 2021, Larson also started her&nbsp;
own podcast with Jessie Ennis, "Learning Lots,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:36.360
which featured guests such as former co-stars&nbsp;
like Jacob Tremblay and Melissa McCarthy.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:41.280
The pandemic even led to her own fans discovering&nbsp;
Larson's musical past after the actress began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:46.860
posting guitar covers on her social media pages,&nbsp;
including "the 1" by Taylor Swift and even her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:51.300
former The Clash at Demonhead cut "Black Sheep."&nbsp;
In a time when Hollywood was stuck in limbo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:56.580
Larson remained ever so relevant online, before&nbsp;
finally making her big-screen return in 2023.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.420
Off-screen, Larson has been&nbsp;
an advocate for social change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.680
and women's rights. This has resulted in some&nbsp;
pretty controversial moments from the actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:09.060
including one instance when she opted not&nbsp;
to applaud Casey Affleck after presenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:13.920
him the Academy Award for best actor in&nbsp;
2017, following sexual assault allegations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.734
against him. When questioned about the&nbsp;
action by Vanity Fair, she asserted,

00:15:17.868 --> 00:15:21.648
"I think that whatever it was that I&nbsp;
did onstage kind of spoke for itself."

00:15:21.648 --> 00:15:26.460
Things got more heated for Larson in 2019,&nbsp;
when, during press for "Captain Marvel," she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:31.380
advocated for more diversity among film critics&nbsp;
and journalists, resulting in the review-bombing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:36.960
of her MCU debut. The criticism has continued&nbsp;
to follow her to this day, despite efforts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:41.820
review aggregate Rotten Tomatoes to deter this&nbsp;
type of targeted trolling. Larson has remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:46.140
vocal about what she believes in, telling&nbsp;
Jane Fonda in an interview with Net-a-Porter,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.234
"It doesn't feel right to be quiet."

00:15:48.234 --> 00:15:53.160
To this day, Larson remains a strong advocate&nbsp;
for social justice. She was one of 300 women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:57.300
who organized the Time's Up initiative to&nbsp;
protest sexual harassment in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:01.320
and has been a vocal supporter of issues&nbsp;
like inclusion, transgender rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:05.640
and law enforcement accountability. Although&nbsp;
her role in the Marvel Cinematic Universe has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.900
her wearing colorful superhero outfits,&nbsp;
it's her activism that's really heroic.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:15.780
2023 is a big year for Larson, especially&nbsp;
with her role as Captain Marvel. She last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:20.220
portrayed the character in the mid-credits&nbsp;
scene of 2021's "Shang-Chi and the Legend of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:25.080
the Ten Rings," directed by her former "Short&nbsp;
Term 12" and "Just Mercy" collaborator Destin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:29.400
Daniel Cretton. She also made a cameo in a&nbsp;
post-credits scene at the end of the Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:34.740
series "Ms. Marvel," teasing her story in&nbsp;
the upcoming 2023 MCU film, "The Marvels."

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:39.900
Most recently, Larson entered the "Fast &amp;&nbsp;
Furious" franchise in "Fast X," joining an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:45.480
ensemble cast that includes Vin Diesel, Jason&nbsp;
Momoa, and Michelle Rodriguez. "Fast X" is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:49.620
relatively small role for Larson compared to&nbsp;
"The Marvels," which finds Carol Danvers taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:54.720
the lead for the first time since 2019's "Captain&nbsp;
Marvel," although this time she won't be alone.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:58.920
"The Marvels" will team up Larson's&nbsp;
Danvers with two other MCU heroines:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:04.980
Iman Vellani's Captain Marvel superfan Kamala Khan&nbsp;
from "Ms. Marvel," as well as Monica Rambeau from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:10.560
2021's "WandaVision," portrayed by Teyonah&nbsp;
Parris. The film finds the trio of heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:15.540
swapping places every time they use their powers,&nbsp;
which may tie back to Carol's past with the Kree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:21.060
It's one of the most highly-anticipated films of&nbsp;
2023, which likely comes as a surprise to Larson.

00:17:21.313 --> 00:17:23.473
"How long will you play Captain Marvel for?"&nbsp;

00:17:23.473 --> 00:17:26.757
"I don't know. I don't know, does&nbsp;
anyone want me to do it again?"

00:17:26.786 --> 00:17:29.306
The short answer: Yes, they do.

